            <div xml:id="d401">

                <pb facs="0590" n="480" xml:id="pg480"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">817.00/5482: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Nicaragua</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Eberhardt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Managua</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-03-19T17:00:00-05:45"><hi rendition="#italic">March 19,
                                1928—5 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 7:11 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>136. With reference to the last sentence of paragraph 2 of the Department’s
                    telegram number 69 of March 15, 7 p.m. [<hi rendition="#italic">noon</hi>]. It
                    is our opinion that it would not be necessary or advisable so far as we can see
                    at present for the President of the United States to issue the suggested order
                    to General McCoy. The latter now has full and general authority to supervise the
                    election and detailed specific instructions might in his opinion hinder his
                    freedom of action.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Eberhardt</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
